                <pb facs="0522" n="510" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg510"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.359 Sanitary/493: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Tuck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-09-07T20:00:00-03:00">September 7, 1940—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>397. Embassy’s 395, September 7 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>], 8 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">9</hi>] p.m. Dr. Melo who has been apprised by the press
                    of the denial issued by the White House has requested that the following message
                    of clarification be transmitted to the President.</p>
                <p>Dr. Melo asserts that at no time during the course of his speech yesterday did he
                    say that President Roosevelt told him that the Senate would ratify the sanitary
                    convention. On the contrary the President has explained to him that this was a
                    time of political unrest and therefore not a suitable one to raise such an
                    issue. Once this time were past, however, he (the President) had felt that an
                    equitable solution could be reached.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tuck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
